erse microbes potently reset mammalian cellular clocks and can drive phase shifts in plants and algae, indicating cross-kingdom effects of microbes on circadian rhythms. In mammals, exposure to soluble bacterial components distinct from canonical innate immune ligands induces acute PER2 upregulation independently of Bmal1 or nascent transcription. A targeted inhibitor screen and biochemical assays implicate p38 MAPK as a modulator of this response. Taken together, this positions bacterial exposure as a previously unrecognized circadian clock input, revealing a new axis of host-microbe interaction that modulates biological timing at the cellular level."}],[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Keywords"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"circadian, clock, microbe, phase, PER2, Streptococcus, E. coli, ligand,"}]],["$","div",null,{"className":"detail-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"className":"button button-primary","href":"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.04.11.717924v1","children":"Article"}],null,null]}]]}]]}],["$","footer",null,{"className":"footer","id":"contact","children":[["$","img",null,{"src":"/assets/kimmeylab/logo/ucsc logo.png","alt":"UC Santa Cruz"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Kimmey Lab"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Department of Microbiology and Environmental Toxicology"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Biomedical Sciences, UC Santa Cruz"}]]}]]}]]}]
